I read the drawing as carbidopa. It has a central quaternary carbon carrying four groups:

- a methyl group on a hashed bond;
- a hydrazine group, NH–NH2;
- a CH2 linked to a 3,4-dihydroxyphenyl ring;
- a carboxylic acid, C(=O)OH.

The ring is a benzene with its two OH groups on adjacent carbons, one para and one meta to the CH2 attachment point.

For the stereocentre, the methyl is hashed, so it points away from the viewer. Seen from the front, the order N (upper right) → CH2 (lower right) → COOH (lower left) runs clockwise. SMILES chirality is read looking from the first neighbour (the methyl) toward the centre, which is from behind the page. From there the same sequence appears counterclockwise, which gives `@` for the neighbour order methyl, N, CH2, COOH.

As a check, the CIP priorities are N > COOH > CH2 > CH3. With the methyl at the back, N → COOH → CH2 runs counterclockwise, so the centre is S, which matches carbidopa.

SMILES: C[C@](NN)(Cc1ccc(O)c(O)c1)C(=O)O

I read this as a danoprevir-type macrocycle and wrote the SMILES from the drawing.

- **Boc carbamate:** a tert-butyl carbamate sits on the macrocycle's NH-bearing carbon.
- **Macrocycle:** a chain of five CH2 groups runs from that carbon to a cis C=C, which leads into a cyclopropane. The ring closes through the cyclopropane quaternary carbon, an amide NH, a proline-type pyrrolidine and a lactam carbonyl.
- **Pyrrolidine:** its hydroxyl-bearing carbon carries an O–C(=O)–N link to a 4-fluoroisoindoline.
- **Cyclopropane substituent:** the quaternary carbon bears a C(=O)NH–SO2–cyclopropyl group.
- **Stereochemistry:** I worked out each centre from its wedge or hash:
  - the Boc-NH carbon is @;
  - the cyclopropane CH is @@;
  - the cyclopropane quaternary carbon is @;
  - the proline α-carbon is @@;
  - the oxygen-bearing pyrrolidine carbon is @.
- **Double bond:** it is drawn cis (Z).

SMILES: CC(C)(C)OC(=O)N[C@H]1CCCCC/C=C\[C@@H]2C[C@]2(NC(=O)[C@@H]3C[C@H](CN3C1=O)OC(=O)N4Cc5cccc(F)c5C4)C(=O)NS(=O)(=O)C6CC6

The drawing shows an anthracycline of the daunorubicin type: a tetracyclic aglycone carrying a daunosamine-type sugar. I read it region by region and then matched the stereochemistry to daunorubicin from memory, so I did not derive every centre from the wedges independently.

- **Aromatic ring:** one methoxy group sits next to the ring-fusion carbon that connects to the lower quinone C=O.
- **Quinone and hydroquinone rings:** two C=O groups and two phenolic OH groups, with the lower OH adjacent to the carbon attached to the C–O–sugar carbon.
- **Saturated ring:**
  - one quaternary carbon bearing OH (wedge) and an acetyl group;
  - one CH2;
  - one CH bearing the glycosidic oxygen (wedge).
- **Sugar ring:** a tetrahydropyran with:
  - an anomeric carbon with a hashed bond to the glycosidic oxygen;
  - a CH2;
  - a CH–NH2 (wedge);
  - a CH–OH (wedge);
  - a CH–CH3 (wedge) next to the ring oxygen.
- **Sugar stereochemistry:** the NH2 and OH wedges are cis, which fits daunorubicin rather than the 4'-epimer, epirubicin.

